---
title: The Boundary
summary: Every island is a serialization boundary. import.meta.og.$ is the one mark that carries anything across it, functions included, into context or props. Stores cross automatically. Refusals fail at your line, not in a serializer.
related:
  - data-state/state-context
  - macros/wire
  - regions/client-islands
---

Each island is its own hydration root. Thus each value that travels from a server-rendered host
into an island — a prop, a context value — crosses a **serialization boundary**. Plain data
crosses with no work. All other values need a way to travel. The rule of ogygia for this is one
sentence:

> **Mark the boundary with `import.meta.og.$()`. Everything that crosses is either data, or
> something that knows how to rebuild itself on the other side.**

## `import.meta.og.$` — the one mark

`og.$` wraps a value at the exact place where it goes to a boundary. What it does depends on
what you give it:

### A function — it crosses *alive*

```svelte
<script>
	import { setContext } from 'ogygia';

	const tax = 0.19; // a local — captured by VALUE

	setContext('fmt', import.meta.og.$((n) => `€${(n * (1 + tax)).toFixed(2)}`));
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- inside any island, plain svelte: -->
<script>
	import { getContext } from 'svelte';
	const fmt = getContext('fmt'); // a REAL, callable function
</script>
<p>{fmt(100)}</p> <!-- €119.00 — synchronous, no eval in production -->
```

The compiler hoists the function. It changes each captured local into a bound value that
crosses as data. The island binds it again. The function itself is **never serialized**. Its
code ships as a build artifact. Its captures ship like any prop. Captures follow prop law:
they must serialize. A captured store crosses live and stays shared — see below.

Two things are build errors, at your file and line:

- a capture from a **server-only module** (`$env/static/private`, `$app/server`,
  `*.server.ts`) — the value would ship into client HTML;
- a mark on something that can never cross (a DOM node, a bare function inside an object) —
  the error fires at the mark, not later inside a serializer.

### Anything else — an explicit, checked crossing

```svelte
<Cart
	items={import.meta.og.$(cartStore)}
	onCheckout={import.meta.og.$((id) => `/checkout/${id}`)}
/>
```

For a non-function value, `og.$` is a **boundary assertion**. The value is classified at that
line. A store passes, and will auto-wire. Plain data passes. A live DOM node or a class
instance without a codec **throws at that line**, with the path inside the value that caused
it. The value itself returns untouched. There is no wrapper to unlearn.

You never *need* `og.$` for values that cross on their own (see the table below). But the mark
is the recommended style. The next reader sees exactly where the page splits, and mistakes
surface at the mark, not at serialize time.

## What crosses, and how

| You pass | How it travels | Needs a mark? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| plain data, `Map`/`Set`/`Date` | serialized (devalue) | no |
| a **store** (`subscribe`-shaped) | value crosses; islands **join to ONE live instance** — `set` in island A repaints island B | no — automatic |
| a **factory store** (custom methods) | value crosses; methods **rebuilt from the factory** on the other side | usually no — provable factories auto-brand; assert with `og.store` (below) in other cases |
| a **class instance** | through its own codec: [`import.meta.og.wire`](/docs/macros/wire) | one time, on the class |
| a **function** | hoisted code plus bound captures (`og.$`) | yes — `og.$` |
| a **snippet** | freezes to HTML, or crosses live (`{#snippet}`) | no |
| a component / UI | it is a [region](/docs/regions/held-regions) — a different noun by intent | `region()` / `<Region>` |
| a DOM node, a bare callback in an object, a secret | **refused**, with the path and a fix | — |

## Stores: automatic, shared, live

```svelte
<script>
	import { setContext } from 'ogygia';
	import { writable } from 'svelte/store';
	setContext('theme', writable('dark'));
</script>
```

Each island that reads `getContext('theme')` gets **the same live store**, not a copy. The
current value crosses at render time. Identity is joined again client-side. Thus writes move
between islands through plain Svelte reactivity. The same store provided under two keys stays
one instance.

### Factory stores

A store with custom methods needs its **factory** on the other side. Methods are code. They
never serialize. Factories that the compiler can prove are branded automatically:

```ts
// auto-branded: the body provably returns a store
export const createCart = (seed = []) => {
	const { subscribe, set, update } = writable(seed);
	return { subscribe, set, update, add: (item) => update((x) => [...x, item]) };
};
```

For a factory it cannot prove (it returns the result of a helper), assert it:

```ts
export const createCart = import.meta.og.store((seed = []) => makeStore(seed));
```

The **first parameter of the factory is the seed** — the value that crosses. Sometimes a
branded store reaches an island whose bundle never loaded the factory. It then degrades to a
plain writable: the value stays, the methods are absent. A console warning names the
one-import fix. It never crashes.

Two store shapes need care. A **`derived`** crosses as a frozen seed. The derivation cannot
travel. Derive again client-side, or share the sources. Store **values** follow prop law: a
callback inside the value of a store is still a bare function.

## Keeping things home: `{ islands: false }`

Not everything must cross. Some context is read only by the server-rendered host: a bag of
element refs, a host-only callback, a very large config object. Keep it native with one
option:

```js
setContext('scrollRefs', refs, { islands: false });
```

Same-tree `getContext` operates as always. Nothing is serialized. In dev, ogygia warns when
the island context payload grows past 32kB, and names the heaviest keys. This option is
usually the fix.

## Policy and errors

The boundary refuses keys that look like secrets (`token`, `apiKey`, …). Thus credentials do
not ride into client HTML. Adjust it one time, server-side:

```ts
// hooks.server.ts
import { configure_boundary } from 'ogygia/internal';

configure_boundary({
	allow: ['tokenColor'],   // a false positive the sniff should let through
	deny: ['sessionBlob']    // a semantic secret the sniff can't see
});
```

Everything that the boundary declines is **explained**. A dropped context key logs the key,
the dot-path to the leaf that caused it, and what to do instead. The design goal: nothing
crosses, or fails to cross, silently.
